    ["A Bouquet of Sevia Daisies"] = "Букет севийских ромашек",
    ["A golden retriever of not-so-pure blood, a freebie from buying a foxhound. Her world was once very simple: sunshine, the courtyard, biscuits, and the little girl who always smiled and squatted down to pat her head. \n Until that bottle of potion was accidentally knocked over. \n Fate never asks for anyone's consent. When the Beyonder characteristic seeped into her fur, blood, bones, and that innocently beating heart, Susie gained her first glimmer of \"consciousness.\" She could understand more—the silence beneath those words, the exhaustion behind the smiles, and the loneliness Audrey felt when alone late at night, which perhaps even Audrey herself hadn't noticed. \n She never hides the fact that she is a dog. This is her pride. Dogs can sniff out lies, while humans can only hear language; dogs can use their tails to tell you all their true feelings, while humans have learned to build walls with smiles. When she lay on the Hall family's carpet, watching the guests come and go, she saw more than any guest—the hypocritical obsequiousness, the calculating eyes, and the jealousy hidden beneath praise. \n She once pondered: As a dog with wisdom, do you feel more like a human or more like a dog? \n She wagged her tail and reached her own answer. \n \"I am more like myself. A golden retriever who learned to speak but still likes to chase her tail, a Beyonder creature who has read about human nature in psychology books but still believes in kindness. There is no need to become human; one can still use a pair of unique eyes to bring a piece of warm peace to all lonely hearts.\" \n \n \"After all, I am just a dog. And that is exactly what I am best at.\""] = "A golden retriever of not-so-pure blood, a freebie from buying a foxhound. Her world was once very simple: sunshine, the courtyard, biscuits, and the little girl who always smiled and squatted down to pat her head. \n Until that bottle of potion was accidentally knocked over. \n Fate never asks for anyone's consent. When the Потусторонний characteristic seeped into her fur, blood, bones, and that innocently beating heart, Susie gained her first glimmer of \"consciousness.\" She could understand more—the silence beneath those words, the exhaustion behind the smiles, and the loneliness Audrey felt when alone late at night, which perhaps even Audrey herself hadn't noticed. \n She never hides the fact that she is a dog. This is her pride. Dogs can sniff out lies, while humans can only hear language; dogs can use their tails to tell you all their true feelings, while humans have learned to build walls with smiles. When she lay on the Hall family's carpet, watching the guests come and go, she saw more than any guest—the hypocritical obsequiousness, the calculating eyes, and the jealousy hidden beneath praise. \n She once pondered: As a dog with wisdom, do you feel more like a human or more like a dog? \n She wagged her tail and reached her own answer. \n \"I am more like myself. A golden retriever who learned to speak but still likes to chase her tail, a Потусторонний creature who has read about human nature in psychology books but still believes in kindness. There is no need to become human; one can still use a pair of unique eyes to bring a piece of warm peace to all lonely hearts.\" \n \n \"After all, I am just a dog. And that is exactly what I am best at.\"",
